T* (And we were silent again until she spoke.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (?I little thought,? said Estella, ?that I should take leave of you in) Tj
T* (taking leave of this spot. I am very glad to do so.?) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (?Glad to part again, Estella? To me, parting is a painful thing. To me,) Tj
T* (the remembrance of our last parting has been ever mournful and) Tj
T* (painful.?) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (?But you said to me,? returned Estella, very earnestly, ??God bless) Tj
T* (you, God forgive you!? And if you could say that to me then, you will) Tj
T* (not hesitate to say that to me now,?now, when suffering has been) Tj
T* (stronger than all other teaching, and has taught me to understand what) Tj
T* (your heart used to be. I have been bent and broken, but?I hope?into a) Tj
T* (better shape. Be as considerate and good to me as you were, and tell me) Tj
T* (we are friends.?) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (?We are friends,? said I, rising and bending over her, as she rose from) Tj
T* (the bench.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (?And will continue friends apart,? said Estella.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (I took her hand in mine, and we went out of the ruined place; and, as) Tj
T* (the morning mists had risen long ago when I first left the forge, so) Tj
T* (the evening mists were rising now, and in all the broad expanse of) Tj
T* (tranquil light they showed to me, I saw no shadow of another parting) Tj
T* (from her.) Tj
